From 283a9c31626d8d1142e896c9489dbfaf4869a6b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 09:37:13 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] NSPasteboard: -clearContents and the object reading and
 writing methods

NSPasteboard had none of the methods macOS 10.6 added for writing and
reading objects but -writeObjects:, which returned NO: -clearContents,
which a program now calls before writing to the general pasteboard,
raised, and so did -readObjectsForClasses:options:,
-canReadObjectForClasses:options: and -pasteboardItems.

On macOS 15, for a pasteboard with an owner and one declared type:
-clearContents tells the owner it has lost the pasteboard, leaves it with
no types and returns the new change count; -writeObjects: of strings, a
pasteboard item or a URL writes the types each gives through
NSPasteboardWriting, without changing the count; -readObjectsForClasses:
makes the strings back from the string type, and nothing from an empty
pasteboard; -canReadObjectForClasses:options: says whether it would; and
-pasteboardItems has one item per object written, none when empty.

-clearContents is -declareTypes:owner: with no types and no owner.
-writeObjects: adds each object's types and writes the property list,
string or data it gives for them (a pasteboard item's own data, as
stored).  Reading takes the first class whose readable types the
pasteboard has, gets the value as its reading options ask (string,
property list, keyed archive or data) and makes the object with
-initWithPasteboardPropertyList:ofType:.  NSString adopts both protocols
for the string type, as on macOS.  GNUstep's pasteboard holds one item,
so where macOS keeps an item per object written, a type two objects give
is written once, by the first, and one object is read; -pasteboardItems
is that item.  URLs, images and colours do not adopt the protocols yet.

The test clears an owned pasteboard and checks the count, writes and
reads a string, writes a pasteboard item with a string and HTML data, and
reads from an empty pasteboard.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
